Yes, exactly! In the bug bounty and security world, breaking a developer's architectural assumption is the very definition of a business logic vulnerability (or logic bug).
Unlike technical bugs (like an old software version flagged by Retire.js), logic bugs cannot be detected by automated scanners because the code itself runs perfectly without crashing. The application is doing exactly what the developer told it to do—the flaw is that what the developer told it to do was based on a broken assumption about reality. [1, 2] 
